module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.argument_order_silently_orders_two_effects

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data argument_order_silently_orders_two_effects: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "argument_order_silently_orders_two_effects" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "INVALID STATE: an application in which two or more actuals -- the callee expression counted as one -- each perform an effect, so the ONLY thing ordering those two effects is the position the author happened to write them in. HARM: the order is an unstated fact. Reordering named arguments is identity-preserving (v2.std.node canonicalize_positional_then_named sorts a named suffix for the content hash, so f(b: eff2(), a: eff1()) and f(a: eff1(), b: eff2()) are one node), yet the two spellings perform their effects in opposite orders. An edit that is a no-op to identity changes behaviour, silently.",
    "WHAT HOLDS TODAY, measured rather than assumed (gunbc MQ-1 PR-B). v2.compiler.eval evaluates an application's actuals in STORED edge order (eval_edge_is_runtime_argument), which is AUTHORED order, and binds them by name through v2.std.arrow_signature application_binding_plan, which maps each declared slot to an INDEX in that authored sequence. So evaluation order is authored order, as it was before named actuals were carried, and nothing regressed: v2.test.claim.named_argument_binding pins it, and a mutation evaluating in binding order reds that claim. The standing rule beside the canonicalizer: evaluation order is never read from a canonicalized node.",
    "WHY THE WALL IS NOT BUILT, stated so the stall is tracked (DESIGN section 4b(2)). The ruled wall (neat-boar-16 via gentle-koi-724, 2026-09-26) is narrow on purpose (DESIGN section 4d): refuse only where argument order is the SOLE thing ordering two effects, counted transitively from real `uses` facts, never from spellings. v2 has no such facts: the v2 dag grammar (v2.extdeps.languages.dag) has no `uses` production, and no v2 stage or v2.std module carries a function's effect requirement -- effects are modeled only in the v1 seed. Refusing every call with two application actuals is decidable today and is the over-prohibition the narrowing exists to avoid; reading v1's effect summary from v2 is a layer inversion. The corpus census that sized the narrowing (a regex over `uses`-declaring fn headers, a floor rather than a count) found about 197 single-effect argument sites and 2 lines with two effectful actuals.",
    "RUNG FOUND AT: outside the ladder's lower rungs only by luck of authored order -- no mechanism refuses the state, so it is mitigatable at best, by the author's own care. CEILING: structurally guaranteed. Membership is decidable once effect requirements are modeled: an application's actuals and callee each carry a transitive effect set, and two non-empty sets under one application with no Bind between them is the refusal, located at the second. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, naming the capability: v2 carries `uses` facts on declared Arrows, transitively summarised per function, readable by v2.compiler.infer at an application. Its controls are already stated: f(a: eff1(), b: eff2()) refuses at b; f(a: eff1(), b: 2) is accepted; get_fn()(read_x()) refuses (the callee is a participant); one effectful actual beside an effectful callee BODY is accepted, because strict evaluation already orders actuals before the body.",
    "THE IDENTITY CLAIM IS FALSE FOR EFFECTFUL APPLICATIONS, and the row names it (neat-boar-16, 2026-09-26). Making named-argument order non-semantic for content identity rests on a purity premise: with effectful actuals, f(a: e1(), b: e2()) and f(b: e2(), a: e1()) hash EQUAL and evaluate in DIFFERENT orders. Until the wall lands, no consumer may reuse or dedupe a result across hash-equal applications on the strength of v2.std.node content_hash when an actual may be effectful.",
    "CENSUS OF CONSUMERS THAT KEY ON A NODE'S content_hash (MQ-1 PR-B), each with its disposition. v2.compiler.eval test_claim_interpretation_cache_digest_authority keys cached claim VERDICTS on the evaluation nodes' digest and did not consult the effect context, so an evaluation with an EffectIoEvalPresent bundle could be cache-Registered and reuse a verdict across two hash-equal effectful applications: FIXED in PR-B, an effectful context is InterpretationCacheIneligible (eval_context_performs_effects). v2.std.materialize marks hash-equal subtrees Share, but its only consumer is v2.std.spine spine_receipt, which COUNTS redundant computations and realizes no reuse (the runner is a later increment): no reuse, the count may over-report effectful sharing, and the runner that acts on Share inherits this row's constraint. v2.lens.affected_set compares before/after hashes to decide what an edit affects: reordering effectful named actuals reads as no change, so an edit that changes effect order can be judged unaffected. Its hash-equality result feeds v2.std.probe_selector (affected_set_rerun_nodes, the rerun selection for test claims), which no non-test module imports at this writing; v2.compiler.self_host.compiler_closure_emit imports only affected_set_closure, a closure walk that compares no hashes. A residual, not on an executing production path, and any consumer that wires probe_selector into selection inherits this row's constraint. v2.std.compilers.compilation_unit (declaration dedupe), v2.std.generic_instantiation, v2.std.compilers.target_model and v2.compiler.translate (type-carrier equality), v2.workflow.operand_flow and locality_affinity (in-run operand identity), v2.extdeps.runtimes.v2_effect_io_pure (locator identity over a reified VALUE, not an application): none reuses an application's result, so no change."
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
